WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.175 --> 00:00:02.550
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Now it's
time to do some job searching.

00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:04.470
Our last video set
up companies and jobs

00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:07.800
using the Google Cloud Talent
solution job search API.

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:11.158
Let's search against the
API and see what comes up.

00:00:11.158 --> 00:00:14.511
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:15.122 --> 00:00:17.330
There's a few things we
should include in the request

00:00:17.330 --> 00:00:18.660
when searching for jobs.

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:21.770
So let's cover those and
then run some searches.

00:00:21.770 --> 00:00:24.830
For every search, we need
to provide request metadata.

00:00:24.830 --> 00:00:26.630
This is an object that
provides information

00:00:26.630 --> 00:00:28.910
about the user making
the search request.

00:00:28.910 --> 00:00:31.010
It's important to provide
these details to ensure

00:00:31.010 --> 00:00:33.980
a consistent user experience
as well as to better train

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:35.480
the machine learning models.

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:37.842
First three properties in
the object are required.

00:00:37.842 --> 00:00:39.800
Domain should be the
domain where the search is

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:42.022
hosted from, such as foo.com.

00:00:42.022 --> 00:00:45.770
Session ID and user ID are
value specific to the user doing

00:00:45.770 --> 00:00:47.420
the search and
should properly be

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:50.780
obfuscated before being
provided, such as hashing them.

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:53.840
That way the API doesn't see
any personally identifiable

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:55.910
information, but
it can tell when

00:00:55.910 --> 00:00:57.890
it's the same session and user.

00:00:57.890 --> 00:01:00.080
Device info is
optional and consists

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:03.280
of the ID and type of device
so you can distinguish create

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:06.240
a web search and an app
search, for example.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:08.810
Allow missing IDs is a Boolean
that makes those first three

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:10.370
fields optional.

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:12.050
When the API is
fully implemented,

00:01:12.050 --> 00:01:13.880
you should always
aim to provide all

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:17.450
of the requested information
to ensure the best results.

00:01:17.450 --> 00:01:19.460
You can use Allow
Missing IDs if you

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:22.520
can't get all the information
or if you're testing.

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:23.960
For our sample
code, we'll provide

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:26.120
some static values for these.

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:28.010
Technically, that's
the only required field

00:01:28.010 --> 00:01:29.250
for doing a search.

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:32.270
But then the request will come
back with all sorts of jobs.

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:35.160
More than likely, we'll want to
actually search for something.

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:37.520
So we'll include a job query.

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:39.470
The job query can
be pretty simple

00:01:39.470 --> 00:01:41.390
with a query string
for the actual search

00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:44.480
terms and different filters
to help narrow down the jobs

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:45.800
that get returned.

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:48.020
For instance, you
can return only jobs

00:01:48.020 --> 00:01:51.860
from a specific company or
filter by employment types.

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:53.790
We'll just stick to the
query string for now.

00:01:53.790 --> 00:01:55.550
But there's more
information about filters

00:01:55.550 --> 00:01:57.600
in the description below.

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:00.350
You can also specify
a job view property,

00:02:00.350 --> 00:02:03.200
which is an enum that declares
how much information you

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:05.810
want to get back with
your job search request.

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:09.380
Job view full gives you back all
the information about each job

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:11.870
result. But you might
want to save some bytes

00:02:11.870 --> 00:02:14.553
and choose another option
that returns less fields.

00:02:14.553 --> 00:02:16.970
Pick the option that makes the
most sense for the platform

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:18.182
you're working on.

00:02:18.182 --> 00:02:19.640
Since you may get
a lot of results,

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:23.090
the API will also paginate, so
you can work with a pagination

00:02:23.090 --> 00:02:24.050
interface.

00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:26.750
We recommend keeping the
page size to 20 or less

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:30.200
to avoid latency issues
and always use pagination

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:32.060
through the API
rather than trying

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:34.190
to load in all the results.

00:02:34.190 --> 00:02:36.510
You'll get a next page
token in the response.

00:02:36.510 --> 00:02:38.420
So if there's more
jobs, you can pass it

00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:41.000
into the next search
queries page token.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:42.380
There's also a
search mode option

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:44.540
that lets you choose
between a normal search

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:46.610
and a featured job search.

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:48.680
If you want to hear
more about feature jobs,

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:50.450
check out the previous
video in the series

00:02:50.450 --> 00:02:52.100
that explains the
difference and when

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:54.230
you should use feature jobs.

00:02:54.230 --> 00:02:55.670
There's some other
optional fields

00:02:55.670 --> 00:02:58.110
you can read about more
in the documentation.

00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:01.218
But let's see some
code and run a search.

00:03:01.218 --> 00:03:03.260
Here's a sample script
that takes a search string

00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:05.090
and then runs a search query.

00:03:05.090 --> 00:03:08.520
We'll set up the API the top
and then we call our function.

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:10.400
Here's where we set
that request metadata

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:12.470
and grab the search
query string.

00:03:12.470 --> 00:03:14.330
As I said before,
the request metadata

00:03:14.330 --> 00:03:17.010
should include as much
information as possible,

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:19.070
but we'll still hash
the user and session

00:03:19.070 --> 00:03:21.830
information to avoid any
personally identifiable

00:03:21.830 --> 00:03:23.390
information.

00:03:23.390 --> 00:03:25.880
Here we build the request
object with our search

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:28.100
term and our request metadata.

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:29.630
We're also explicitly
calling out

00:03:29.630 --> 00:03:32.840
that this is a normal job search
instead of a feature job search

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:34.580
and saying we want
the full information

00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:36.170
for each job returned.

00:03:36.170 --> 00:03:37.705
We've also got a
page size for 20

00:03:37.705 --> 00:03:41.390
for now, though we'd have to
use page tokens or an offset

00:03:41.390 --> 00:03:43.580
if we wanted to get
to the next page.

00:03:43.580 --> 00:03:46.160
With that, we'll make
the call with jobs.search

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:48.020
and we'll pass in our object.

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:50.840
Since we used job view full,
we'll get a lot of details

00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:51.380
back.

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:54.560
But the code is only going to
look for two things, the title

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:56.450
and the search text snippet.

00:03:56.450 --> 00:03:58.190
Each result will
return the field

00:03:58.190 --> 00:03:59.960
that your job use specifies.

00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:03.168
And that may be getting back
a job object for each result

00:04:03.168 --> 00:04:05.460
as well as the fields that
are specific to the results,

00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:07.220
such as the snippet.

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:10.700
Now, if we run the code
and we search for engineer,

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:13.130
we'll get some results in
the matching jobs field.

00:04:13.130 --> 00:04:15.380
By default, these are
ordered from relevance

00:04:15.380 --> 00:04:16.980
by the ML models.

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:18.980
You can specify the order
in the search request

00:04:18.980 --> 00:04:20.940
if you need to do
some custom logic.

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:23.270
But we recommend using
the default relevance.

00:04:23.270 --> 00:04:25.520
This is a great starting
point for getting data loaded

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:28.520
into the system and performing
some actual searches to see

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:30.020
what kind of results you get.

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:31.920
Looking at the raw
search response,

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:34.460
you'll see some important
additional information.

00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:36.612
Total size and
estimated total size

00:04:36.612 --> 00:04:38.570
can help you with
pagination, and matching jobs

00:04:38.570 --> 00:04:40.760
contain the actual
search results.

00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:42.470
In the metadata
section, you'll also

00:04:42.470 --> 00:04:46.440
get a request ID that's unique
to this specific search.

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:48.530
These are globally
unique IDs generated

00:04:48.530 --> 00:04:50.640
for that specific request.

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:53.510
So if you're seeing an
issue or have a question,

00:04:53.510 --> 00:04:55.610
these requests IDs
can be very helpful

00:04:55.610 --> 00:04:57.378
if you need to contact support.

00:04:57.378 --> 00:04:59.420
From here, you should be
able to load information

00:04:59.420 --> 00:05:02.350
into the job search API
and start doing searches.

00:05:02.350 --> 00:05:03.860
In our next video,
I'll talk more

00:05:03.860 --> 00:05:06.020
about the specifics of
relevance and ranking

00:05:06.020 --> 00:05:07.587
for the job search results.

00:05:07.587 --> 00:05:08.420
Thanks for watching.

00:05:08.420 --> 00:05:10.330
And remember, when
looking for talent,

00:05:10.330 --> 00:05:12.590
it's OK to keep your
head in the Cloud.

00:05:12.590 --> 00:05:16.540
[MUSIC PLAYING]

